\description{
Monte Carlo sensitivity analysis for the statistical power of the
\emph{t}-test of a targeted regression coefficient. Given a planned
(\code{supposed_*}) covariance structure and a true (\code{true_*})
covariance structure, the function draws \code{G} replications,
fits the multiple regression, and reports the empirical proportion of
replications on which the \emph{t}-test of the targeted coefficient
rejects, together with the realized distribution of the estimated
unstandardized coefficient \eqn{\hat b_j}, its standard error, and the
test statistic. \code{ss_power_reg_coef_sensitivity()}
is the power-oriented sibling of \code{\link{ss_aipe_reg_coef_sensitivity}}
(which is CI-width oriented).
}
\details{
When the supposed and true covariance structures are identical, the
function performs a Monte Carlo evaluation of the planning procedure
(no misspecification); when they differ, it performs a sensitivity
analysis on the consequences of misspecifying the population
covariance structure for the targeted coefficient's power. The planning
step calls \code{\link{ss_power_reg_coef}} with the supposed
covariance structure; the simulation step generates data from the true
covariance structure.

The coefficient is the unstandardized one, \eqn{b_j}, the \emph{j}th
element of \eqn{\Sigma_{XX}^{-1}\sigma_{XY}}, from \code{true_cov_XX}
and \code{true_cov_YX} for the true value and from their supposed
counterparts for the supposed value, and every replication is fit in the
units of the data. The \emph{t} statistic of a coefficient is the same
whether or not the data are standardized, so the power is that of the
test of the standardized coefficient as well; the standardized
coefficient itself, \eqn{b_j \sigma_{X_j}/\sigma_Y} (Kelley & Maxwell,
2003, p. 316), and its precision are the subject of
\code{\link{ss_aipe_src_sensitivity}}.
}
\section{The record of the simulation}{

Every sensitivity function of the package summarizes its replications
over one denominator: the replications that produced a confidence
interval (for a power sensitivity function, a test). The proportion of
intervals that met the width target (\code{pct_ci_less_width}), the
proportions of intervals that missed the true value, and the summaries
of the widths are all taken over those replications. A replication
without an interval is in none of these rows. It is counted, and the
returned table carries the counts in its attribute \code{simulation},
a list read with \code{attr(x, "simulation")}:
\describe{
  \item{\code{attempted}}{the number of replications asked for
    (\code{G}).}
  \item{\code{returned}}{the number that produced an interval (a
    test).}
  \item{\code{failed}}{the number that did not
    (\code{attempted - returned}).}
  \item{\code{denominator}}{the number of replications the rows named
    above are taken over, which is \code{returned}.}
  \item{\code{width_target_probability}}{the proportion of the
    replications with an interval whose interval met the width target,
    the value of the \code{pct_ci_less_width} row; \code{NA} for a
    function or a call without a width target, and when no replication
    produced an interval.}
  \item{\code{width_target_mc_ci}}{the exact binomial
    (Clopper-Pearson) limits for that proportion, at the confidence
    level of the table. They describe the error of the simulation, which
    shrinks as \code{G} grows, and nothing else.}
}
A function that records more about its run adds elements to the list,
and its help page names them. The line "Replications" beneath the
printed table gives \code{G}, and the three counts when a replication
was without an interval. With many replications without an interval the
rows describe only the studies in which the interval could be formed,
so \code{failed} is to be reported beside them.
}

